WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.400
After supervised learning, the most widely used form of machine learning is unsupervised

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:08.400
learning.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.240
Let's take a look at what that means.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:16.800
We've talked about supervised learning, and this video is about unsupervised learning.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.080
But don't let the name unsupervised fool you.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.120
Unsupervised learning is, I think, just as super as supervised learning.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.380
When we're looking at supervised learning in the last video, we call the look something

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:30.440
like this.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:35.400
In the case of a classification problem, each example was associated with an output label

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:41.720
Y, such as benign or malignant, designated by the O's and crosses.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:48.560
In unsupervised learning, we're given data that isn't associated with any output labels

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:55.520
Y. Say you're given data on patients and their tumor size and the patient's age, but

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:59.360
not whether the tumor was benign or malignant.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.520
So the data set looks like this on the right.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:10.620
We're not asked to diagnose whether the tumor is benign or malignant, because we're not

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.040
given any labels Y in the data set.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:19.720
Instead our job is to find some structure or some pattern, or just find something interesting

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.120
in the data.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.560
This is unsupervised learning.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.400
We call it unsupervised because we're not trying to supervise the algorithm to give

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.840
some quote, right answer for every input.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:37.760
Instead, we asked the algorithm to figure out all by itself, what's interesting, or what

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.200
patterns or structures there might be in this data.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.720
With this particular data set, an unsupervised learning algorithm might decide that the

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:52.240
data can be assigned to two different groups or two different clusters.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:02:00.600
And so it might decide that there's one cluster or group over here, and there's another cluster

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.480
or group over here.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:09.440
This is a particular type of unsupervised learning called a clustering algorithm, because it places

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.760
the unlabeled data into different clusters.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.440
And this turns out to be used in many applications.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.800
For example, clustering is used in Google News.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.160
What Google News does is every day it goes and looks at hundreds of thousands of news articles

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.080
on the internet and groups related stories together.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.920
For example, here's a sample from Google News, where the headline of the top article

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.160
is Giant Panda Gives Birth to Rear Twin Clubs at Japan's Oldest Zoo.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.640
This article had actually caught my eye because my daughter loves pandas.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:52.000
And so there are a lot of stuff, panda toys and watching of panda videos in my halls.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:59.800
And looking at this, you might notice that below this are other related articles.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:06.160
Maybe from the headlines alone, you can start to guess what clustering might be doing.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:14.600
Notice that the word panda appears here, here, here, here, and here.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:21.840
And notice that the word twin also appears in all five articles.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.240
And the word zoo also appears in all of these articles.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:31.200
So the clustering algorithm is finding articles out of all the hundreds of thousands of news

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.880
articles on the internet that day, finding the articles that mention similar words and

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.520
grouping them into clusters.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.360
Now what's cool is that this clustering algorithm figures out on its own which words suggest that

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.800
certain articles are in the same group.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.240
What I mean is there isn't an employee at Google News who's telling the algorithm to

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:57.960
find articles that the word panda and twins and zoo to put them into the same cluster.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.120
The news topics change every day.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:05.280
And there are so many news stories, it just isn't feasible to have people doing this

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.880
every single day for all the topics the news covers.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:15.520
Instead, the algorithm has to figure out on its own without supervision what are the clusters

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.760
of news articles today.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:23.800
So that's why this clustering algorithm is a type of unsupervised learning algorithm.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:28.200
Let's look at a second example of unsupervised learning applied to clustering

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.600
genetic or DNA data.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.120
This image shows a picture of DNA microarray data.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:41.920
These look like tiny grids of a spreadsheet and each tiny column represents the genetic

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:45.400
or DNA activity of one person.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:52.120
So for example, this entire column here is from one person's DNA and this other column

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.640
is of another person.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:57.960
Each row represents a particular gene.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:04.280
So just as an example, perhaps this row here might represent a gene that affects eye color

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:09.720
or this row here is a gene that affects how tall someone is.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:14.720
Researchers have even found a genetic link to whether someone dislikes certain vegetables

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.160
such as broccoli or Brussels sprouts or asparagus.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:23.160
So next time someone asks you why didn't you finish your salad, you can tell them,

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.760
oh, maybe it's genetic.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:32.320
For DNA microarrays, the idea is to measure how much certain genes are expressed for each

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:33.880
individual person.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:40.000
So these colors, red, green, gray, and so on, show the degree to which different individuals

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.800
do or do not have a specific gene active.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:51.200
And what you can do is then run a clustering algorithm to group individuals into different

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.400
categories or different types of people.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:00.080
Like maybe these individuals are grouped together and let's just call this type one.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.800
And these people are grouped into type two.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.280
And these people are grouped as type three.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.840
This is unsupervised learning because we're not telling the algorithm in advance that there

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:19.760
is a type one person with certain characteristics or a type two person with certain characteristics.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.240
Instead what we're saying is, here's a bunch of data.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.640
I don't know what the different types of people are, but can you automatically find

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:32.200
structure into data and automatically figure out what are the major types of individuals?

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:36.840
Since we're not giving the algorithm the right answer for the examples in advance,

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.640
this is unsupervised learning.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.640
Here's a third example.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:46.480
Many companies have huge databases of customer information.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:52.000
Given this data, can you automatically group your customers into different market segments

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.520
so that you can more efficiently serve your customers?

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:01.040
Now briefly, the deep learning.ai team did some research to better understand the deep

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:07.280
learning.ai community and why different individuals take these classes, subscribe to the batch

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:12.000
weekly newsletter, or attend our Py in AI events.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.600
Let's visualize the deep learning.ai community as this collection of people.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:25.440
Running clustering, that is market segmentation, found a few distinct groups of individuals.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:30.840
Some groups primary motivation is seeking knowledge to grow their skills.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.520
Perhaps this is you, and so that's great.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:38.400
A second group's primary motivation is looking for a way to develop their career.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:42.440
Maybe you want to get a promotion or a new job or make some career progression.

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:45.520
If this describes you, that's great too.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:51.400
And yet another group wants to stay updated on how AI impacts their field of work.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:53.120
Perhaps this is you.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:54.560
That's great too.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:08:00.360
This is a clustering that our team use to try to better serve our community as we're

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:06.840
trying to figure out what are the major categories of learners in the deep learning.ai community.

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:10.400
So if any of these is your top motivation for learning, that's great.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.760
And I hope I'll be able to help you on your journey.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:19.920
Or in case this is you, and you want something totally different than the other three categories,

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:20.920
that's fine too.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:24.480
And I want you to know, I love you all the same.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:29.640
So to summarize, a clustering algorithm, which is type of unsupervised learning algorithm,

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:35.520
takes data without labels and tries to automatically group them into clusters.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:41.000
And so maybe the next time you see or think of a panda, maybe you think of clustering

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.520
as well.

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:47.360
And besides clustering, there are other types of unsupervised learning as well.

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:51.200
Let's go on to the next video to take a look at some other types of unsupervised

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:52.200
learning algorithms.
